is four tiles, they need to have a pair and two more melds, but four tiles can't form that. Unless there's a special pattern.\n\nLooking at the user's hand before Penging: [T2, T7x2, B7, F2, F3, J3]. After Penging T7, they have three T7s (Pung), and the remaining tiles would be T2, B7, F2, F3, J3. Wait, that's five tiles. Then they have to discard one, making four. So possible discard options: T2, B7, F2, F3, J3. Let's say they discard T2. Now the hand is B7, F2, F3, J3. These four tiles don't form any melds or a special pattern. They are all single tiles. So this is not a valid hand. Therefore, Penging T7 would not help them form a winning hand.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard B7, the hand is T2, F2, F3, J3. Still no melds. Same issue. So Penging T7 seems unhelpful.\n\nNow, what if they don't Peng and just pass? Then their hand remains as is. Let's see if they can form a winning hand by drawing or discarding. Their current hand is [T2, T7x2, B7, F2, F3, J3] plus two melds. They need to form four melds and a pair, or a special pattern.\n\nThey have two Chows already. They need two more melds and a pair. The current hand has T2, T7x2, B7, F2, F3, J3. Let's see:\n\n- T2 could be part of a Chow if they have T1 and T3, but they don't. They have T2, but no T1 or T3. So maybe not.\n\n- T7x2: they have two T7s. Could form a pair. Then they need two more melds. The B7 is a single tile. F2, F3, J3 are singles. So if they have a pair of T7s, then they need two more melds. But the remaining tiles are B7, F2, F3, J3. These don't form any melds. So that's not possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they have a special pattern. Let's check the scoring patterns. For example, All Honors? No, they have T2 and B7, which are suited tiles. All Terminals? No, they have T2, B7, which are not terminals. Seven Pairs? They have T7x2, but others are singles. Not enough pairs. Chicken Hand? That's a hand with no fans, but they might have some points. But the user needs at least 8 points to win.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a hand with All Chows. But they have T2, T7x2, B7, etc. Not likely.\n\nSo it seems like the user's current hand isn't in a good position to win. The only option is to Peng T7, but that leaves them with four mismatched tiles. Therefore, the best move is to Pass, as Penging T7 doesn't help and might even make it harder to form a winning hand.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The user has two Chows and needs two more melds or a special pattern. Penging T7 forms a Pung but leaves four mismatched tiles. Passing allows maintaining current hand structure for potential future draws.]\nReason: [Penging T7 creates a Pung but results in four unconnected tiles (B7, F2, F3, J3) that cannot form valid melds or patterns. Passing preserves the current hand's flexibility for future draws or discards to complete a winning hand.]\nAnswer: Pass"}
